  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 256 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unsettled pattern prevails today through the \nmiddle of the  week, with near average temperatures, increasingly \nhumid conditions, and daily chances of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nA low pressure system is currently sliding across the Ohio River \nValley, with an associated cold front draped across the Tennessee \nValley through the South. Just to the west/southwest of the area, \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms have been training across the \narea, bringing localized rainfall totals in some areas to 4-8\". \nMeanwhile across our forecast area, KAKQ radar is only detecting a \nfew light showers across the western piedmont. With the low to the \nwest, and high pressure currently offshore, moisture is slowly being \nfunneled into across the region. SPC mesoscale analysis still \ndepicts that PW across most of the AKQ FA is still 1.5\" or less. A \ntongue of moisture can be seen making its way up into southern VA \nwhere the high QPE is being observed. High PW values will continue \nto overspread the area today, with 2.2-2.4\" forecast starting this \nevening into tonight as the deep-layered S-SW flow advects rich Gulf \nmoisture northwards. The highest precip chances will initially be \nacross south-central VA during the afternoon (as the deep moisture \narrives here first). Showers and tstms should gradually spread NE \nthrough the evening (though it may very well remain dry near the \nimmediate coast and on the Eastern Shore). While localized flash-\nflooding is possible if storms train across train across the same \nlocation (as seen this morning to our west), these storms will be \nmoving and are not expected to be stationary. With this in mind and \nthe fact that hi-res guidance is not honing in on a certain area, \nhave decided to not issue a Flood Watch for any of the area at this \ntime. SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk for severe storms for areas \ngenerally west of I-95 for today, with isolated damaging wind gusts \npossible. Temperatures today will be in the mid 80s across the \npiedmont due to the increased cloud cover and shower/thunderstorm \ndevelopment, while the remainder of the area will like reach the \nupper 80s to near 90 degrees this afternoon.\n\nShowers/tstms are likely across the area on Monday afternoon as a \nlead shortwave potentially tracks over the area. Some guidance is \nnot quite as excited about Monday, while others still suggest that \nthis will be the most widespread day for convection. The trough \ndampens a bit from Tuesday/Wednesday, but scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are still expected during the aftn/evening on both \ndays as SW flow aloft weakens some and deep moisture remains nearby. \nGiven the environment, and relatively weak unidirectional flow aloft \nand tall/skinny CAPE, there will be a threat of localized flash \nflooding each day, but the severe threat should remain relatively \nlow with these thunderstorms due to the very saturated atmospheric \ncolumn and weak flow aloft. Days 1-3 (Sunday through Tuesday) are in \na 'Marginal' ERO as the overall flash flood risk is more isolated \ngiven a lack of a larger scale focusing mechanism, as the front \nassociated with the low pressure system remains north of the local \narea. High temperatures dip down slightly below average during the \nearly week period thanks to the cloud cover and multiple rounds of \nshowers/tstms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more typical summertime pattern returns mid-late \nweek, with isolated to scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms \nand near to slightly above normal temperatures through the weekend.\n\nThough not quite as unsettled as the first half of the week, the \nlatter half of the week will see isolated to scattered afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms. With the decreased convective coverage \nforecast, temperatures will return to near to slightly above normal \nwith highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Dew points will remain in \nthe mid to upper 70s, making for quite humid conditions. Heat \nindices will approach 100 degrees for a good portion of the area \nThursday through Saturday, but due to temperatures remaining near \nnormal, heat headlines are not expected at this time.",
